.TH POW 3 "2026-05-14" "Substrate" "Library Functions Manual"
.SH NAME
pow, powf, powl, sqrt, sqrtf, sqrtl, cbrt, cbrtf, cbrtl, hypot,
hypotf, hypotl, rsqrt, pown, powr, rootn, compound \- power and
root functions
.SH LIBRARY
Math library (libm, \-lm)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B #include <math.h>
.PP
.BI "double pow(double " x ", double " y );
.br
.BI "float powf(float " x ", float " y );
.br
.BI "long double powl(long double " x ", long double " y );
.PP
.BI "double sqrt(double " x );
.br
.BI "float sqrtf(float " x );
.br
.BI "long double sqrtl(long double " x );
.PP
.BI "double cbrt(double " x );
.br
.BI "float cbrtf(float " x );
.br
.BI "long double cbrtl(long double " x );
.PP
.BI "double hypot(double " x ", double " y );
.br
.BI "float hypotf(float " x ", float " y );
.br
.BI "long double hypotl(long double " x ", long double " y );
.PP
.BI "double rsqrt(double " x );
.br
.BI "double pown(double " x ", intmax_t " n );
.br
.BI "double powr(double " x ", double " y );
.br
.BI "double rootn(double " x ", int " n );
.br
.BI "double compound(double " x ", intmax_t " n );
.SH DESCRIPTION
The
.BR pow ()
function returns the value of
.IR x
raised to the power of
.IR y .
.PP
The
.BR sqrt (),
.BR cbrt (),
and
.BR rsqrt ()
functions return the square root, cube root, and reciprocal square
root (
.RI 1 " /" " " sqrt( x )
) of
.IR x .
.PP
The
.BR hypot ()
function returns
.RI sqrt( x "^2 + " y ^2)
without spurious overflow or underflow when one argument is much
larger than the other.
.PP
The C23
.BR pown ()
function raises
.I x
to an integer power
.I n
using binary exponentiation; unlike
.BR pow (),
the sign of the result for negative
.I x
is unambiguous (\-1 if
.I n
is odd, +1 if even).
.PP
The
.BR powr ()
function computes
.RI x ^ y
via
.RI exp( y " * ln(" x ))
and is defined only for
.I x
.BR ">= 0;"
unlike
.BR pow ()
it deliberately returns NaN for several boundary cases (
.RB powr(0,\ 0),
.RB powr(\(+-inf,\ 0),
.RB powr(1,\ \(+-inf)
) where the limit doesn't exist or isn't unique.
.PP
The
.BR rootn ()
function returns the
.IR n -th
root of
.IR x .
.RB rootn( x ,\ 2)
is
.BR sqrt( x ),
.RB rootn( x ,\ 3)
is
.BR cbrt( x ).
A negative
.I n
gives the reciprocal of the corresponding positive-root call.
.PP
The
.BR compound ()
function returns
.RI (1\ +\  x )^ n ,
implemented as
.RI exp( n " * log1p(" x ))
to remain accurate for small
.I x
(financial compounding patterns).
.SH RETURN VALUE
.SS pow(x, y) — C99 Annex F.9.4.4 special cases
The following are all returned exactly, ignoring rounding:
.RS 4
.nf
pow(x, \(+-0)         = 1     for any x, including NaN and infinities
pow(\(+-1, y)         = 1     for any y, including NaN
pow(+1, NaN)        = 1
pow(\(+-0, y > 0)     = \(+-0    sign follows x if y is an odd integer
pow(\(+-0, y < 0)     = \(+-inf  pole error; raises FE_DIVBYZERO
pow(x < 0, frac)    = NaN   domain error; raises FE_INVALID
pow(\(+-inf, y > 0)   = \(+-inf
pow(\(+-inf, y < 0)   = \(+-0
pow(x, \(+-inf)       = 0/inf depending on |x| < 1, > 1, or == 1
pow(NaN, y != 0)    = NaN
pow(x, NaN)         = NaN
.fi
.RE
.SS sqrt(x), cbrt(x), hypot(x, y), rsqrt(x)
On a normal positive input
.BR sqrt ()
and
.BR cbrt ()
return the positive root.
.BR cbrt (\-x)
returns
.BR \-cbrt( x ).
.PP
.BR sqrt (\-0)
returns \-0.
.BR sqrt (\-1)
and any negative input raises
.BR FE_INVALID ,
sets
.I errno
to
.BR EDOM ,
and returns NaN.
.PP
.BR rsqrt (0)
raises
.BR FE_DIVBYZERO ,
sets
.I errno
to
.BR ERANGE ,
and returns +infinity.
.BR rsqrt (\-1)
behaves the same way as
.BR sqrt (\-1).
.PP
.BR hypot ()
returns +infinity whenever either argument is infinite, even if the
other is NaN \(em this matches IEEE 754 because the infinite leg
dominates any finite or NaN second leg.
.SH ERRORS
.TP
.B EDOM
A domain error occurred.  Specifically:
.BR pow ()
called with negative
.I x
and non-integer
.IR y ;
.BR sqrt ()
or
.BR rsqrt ()
called with negative
.IR x ;
.BR powr ()
called with negative
.IR x .
.TP
.B ERANGE
A range error occurred.  Specifically:
.BR pow ()
result overflows the
.I double
range;
.BR pow (0,\ y)
with negative
.IR y ;
.BR rsqrt (0).
.SH EXAMPLES
.PP
Computing the Euclidean distance between two points:
.PP
.RS
.nf
#include <math.h>
double distance(double x1, double y1, double x2, double y2) {
    return hypot(x2 \- x1, y2 \- y1);
}
.fi
.RE
.PP
Note that
.BR hypot ()
is used rather than
.BR sqrt(dx*dx + dy*dy) ;
the latter overflows whenever
.B dx
or
.B dy
exceeds about
.RB sqrt(DBL_MAX) ,
while
.BR hypot ()
remains correct up to the representable range of either coordinate.
.PP
Integer exponentiation that handles negative bases correctly:
.PP
.RS
.nf
double signed_pow(double base, int exponent) {
    return pown(base, exponent);
}
/* signed_pow(\-2.0, 3) == \-8.0 (pow would also return \-8.0 in
 * this case, but pow(\-2.0, 3.5) is NaN while pown is undefined). */
.fi
.RE
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR exp (3),
.BR log (3),
.BR math (7)
.SH STANDARDS
C99, POSIX.1\-2001.  C23 added
.BR pown (),
.BR powr (),
.BR rootn (),
.BR compound (),
and
.BR rsqrt ().
